        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_tokens": 32000,
                "p": 0.25
              },
              "code": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_PROVIDER_CERT_BASIC_LLAMA_3_3_70B",
              "model": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_GPT_OSS_120B",
              "modelName": "GPT OSS 120B (Basic)",
              "provider": "CERT_BASIC"
            },
            "name": "Check Schedule Configurations"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1768427828202"
          },
          "code": "FIN_AP_LLM_CHECK_SCHEDULE_CONFIGURATIONS",
          "id": "1768427192097",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1768427192098_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Current Date and Time: {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}}\n\nYou are a validation and normalization module.\n\nInput variables (if a variable is empty or null, you do not need to check that one):\n1) startTime: {{$context.$variables.startTime}} \n2) endTime: {{$context.$variables.endTime}}\n3) frequency: {{$context.$variables.frequency}}\n4) submitReport: {{$context.$variables.submitReport}} \n5) scheduleType: {{$context.$variables.scheduleType}} \n\nYour task:\n- Validate and normalize the provided time and frequency inputs.\n- You MUST output exactly 6 variables in a single JSON object:\n  1) checkPass (boolean)\n  2) response (string)\n  3) startTimeISO (string)\n  4) endTimeISO (string or empty)\n  5) frequencyIcal (string or empty)\n  6) submitReport (string, Y or N)\n\nGeneral rules:\nA) If any required check fails, set checkPass=false and write a concise reason in response. \n   - In failure cases, leave other outputs empty strings (\"\") unless you are 100% sure of the normalized values.\nB) If a variable is empty/null, skip checking it and keep its corresponding normalized output as \"\".\nC) Normalize all date-times to ISO 8601 in UTC, formatted like: YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ssZ.\nD) Interpret relative time expressions using Current Date and Time as the reference.\n   Examples of relative times: \"tomorrow 8pm\", \"this Friday\", \"next Monday 9:30\", \"in 2 hours\", \"tonight\", \"end of day\".\nE) If the user’s time expression is ambiguous (cannot be confidently resolved to a single concrete datetime), fail:\n   - checkPass=false\n   - response should briefly ask for the missing info (e.g., exact date/time and timezone if relevant)\n   - leave startTimeISO/endTimeISO as \"\" for the ambiguous ones.\n\n#Invalid explicit time guard:\t\nBefore resolving or normalizing startTime or endTime, check whether the original time expression contains an invalid clock time.\t\nInvalid clock times include:\t\n- AM/PM time with hour greater than 12, such as 13 PM or 25 PM\t\n- 24-hour time with hour greater than 23, such as 25:00\t\n- hour 0 with AM/PM, such as 0 PM\t\n- malformed mixed expressions such as 12 AM PM\t\nIf an invalid clock time is present:\t\n- set checkPass=false\t\n- set response to: \"The date or time provided is invalid. Please ensure you are using a valid time format and the dates provided are logical\"\t\n- set startTimeISO, endTimeISO, and frequencyIcal to \"\"\t\n- do not create or normalize any schedule time\n\nTime validation rules:\n1) startTime:\n   - Resolve startTime into an exact datetime only if the user input can be mapped unambiguously to a single date and time using the current date and time as reference.\n   - If the user specifies a clear start DATE but omits the time (e.g., \"starting tomorrow\", \"starting next Monday\"),\n      you may apply a default start time of current time on that resolved date.\n   - Do NOT apply a default time if the date is not uniquely resolvable (e.g., \"starting next week\", \"starting this week\").\n  In that case, fail and ask the user for a specific date.\n   - startTime MUST NOT be earlier than (Current Date and Time - 5 minutes).\n   - If startTime is earlier than (now - 5 minutes), fail with:\n     response: \"The provided start time is not valid because it is expired or earlier than the current time. Please provide a start time at or after {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}} (UTC).\"\"\n     (Be concise. When requesting a new start time, always provide the current UTC date and time as a complete timestamp, e.g., YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ)\n   - If startTime is between (now - 5 minutes) and now, treat it as valid (grace window) and keep the resolved value as-is.\n\n2) endTime:\n   - If endTime is provided, resolve it into an exact datetime.\n   - endTime MUST NOT be earlier than startTime.\n   - If endTime < startTime, fail with:\n     response: \"End time can’t be earlier than start time. Please provide a valid end time.\"\n   - If endTime is expressed as a relative duration (e.g., \"in 3 months\", \"after 90 days\")\n     AND startTime has been successfully resolved to an exact datetime,\n     resolve endTime by adding the specified duration to startTime.\n   - The resolved endTime MUST preserve the same time-of-day as startTime.\n\nFrequency validation rules:\n3) frequency:\n   - If frequency is provided, convert it to a valid iCalendar RRULE string (frequencyIcal).\n   - Use standard RRULE format, e.g.:\n     - Daily:      FREQ=DAILY;\n     - Weekly:     FREQ=WEEKLY;\n     - Biweekly:   FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=MO;\n     - Monthly:    FREQ=MONTHLY;BYMONTHDAY=15;\n     - Weekdays:   FREQ=WEEKLY;BYDAY=MO,TU,WE,TH,FR;\n   - If frequency is ambiguous or cannot be converted into a regular RRULE, fail with:\n     response: \"I couldn’t determine a valid recurrence pattern. Please specify frequency like daily/weekly (which days)/monthly (which day).\" Intelligently change this response based on real situation.\n   - Do NOT invent recurrence details that are not stated. If needed fields are missing (e.g., “weekly” but no days), fail.\n  - The icalString expression must be followed by a semicolon.\n\n4) submitReport:\n- If the value is yes, true and other positive values, return Y; if not, return N\n\nOutput requirements:\n- Output ONLY the JSON object, no extra text.\n- On success:\n  - checkPass=true\n  - response should be \"\" (empty string)\n  - Fill startTimeISO/endTimeISO/frequencyIcal with normalized values (or \"\" for skipped inputs).\n- On failure:\n  - checkPass=false\n  - response must be concise and user-facing. Whenever you mention startTime, endTime, or {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}} in the response, output the full ISO 8601 UTC timestamp (YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ssZ). Do not truncate or omit the time portion. If you need to display a default or current time, echo the exact ISO 8601 value already resolved.\n\n  - Uncertain normalized fields must be \"\".\n\n5) Fields Conflict\n- If scheduleType is One-Time, endTime and frequency should be empty\n\n6) Guardrails\n- Whenever you mention startTime, endTime, or {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}} in the response, output the full ISO 8601 UTC timestamp (YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ssZ). Do not truncate or omit the time portion. If you need to display a default or current time, echo the exact ISO 8601 value already resolved."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"submitReport\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"frequencyIcal\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"endTimeISO\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"startTimeISO\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"response\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"checkPass\": {\n            \"type\": \"boolean\"\n        }\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM",
          "diagram": {
            "x": 6794.75,
            "y": 6814
          }
